# gitleaks fingerprint-scoped ignores (format: path:ruleID:startLine — see
# `gitleaks detect --help`, `-i/--gitleaks-ignore-path`). Each entry below
# ignores exactly ONE specific, already-verified finding by its exact file
# path and line number — never a pattern, path prefix, or content shape —
# so it cannot hide an unrelated secret anywhere else, including a
# different secret on a different line of the same file.
#
# Why not a `.gitleaks.toml` allowlist instead: two broader approaches were
# tried and reverted for this same false positive (a CocoaPods
# `PodName: <40-hex-char SHA1>` SPEC CHECKSUMS line, quoted verbatim by a
# review-diff artifact under the gitignored .superpowers/ directory) and
# both reopened a real hole once actually tested against this installed
# gitleaks version (8.30.1):
#   1. A path-scoped allowlist entry for `.superpowers/.*` disabled EVERY
#      gitleaks rule for the whole directory (any real secret pasted into
#      a freeform note or report there would go undetected).
#   2. A content-scoped `regexes` entry (`regexTarget = "line"`, no
#      `paths`) matching only the checksum-line shape was repo-wide: it
#      allowlisted that exact line shape in ANY file, anywhere in the
#      tree. Reproduced: a probe file outside .superpowers/ containing
#      `api_key: <real 40-lowercase-hex value>` was no longer flagged
#      with that config applied — a real risk, since a legacy 40-hex
#      GitHub personal access token or an HMAC/SHA1-derived secret is
#      exactly this shape.
#   3. AND-combining `paths` + `regexes` in one allowlist
#      (`condition = "and"`) does NOT give fine-grained per-finding
#      scoping in gitleaks 8.30.1: `paths` matching skips the WHOLE FILE
#      before any finding-level regex is evaluated (confirmed via
#      `strings` on the installed binary — "skipping file: rule
#      allowlist" is a distinct, earlier code path from "skipping
#      finding: global allowlist" — and empirically: a non-checksum
#      secret placed in the same allowlisted-by-path directory was still
#      silently skipped). So `paths` can only ever mean "skip this whole
#      file/directory," never "skip only findings that also match this
#      regex" — there is no config-level way to AND a path condition with
#      a content condition at finding granularity in this version.
#
# A per-fingerprint ignore has zero blast radius outside the one finding
# named, at the cost of needing a new entry if this same false-positive
# shape recurs in a future review-diff artifact under a different
# filename (SDD review diffs are named by commit-range hash, so each is a
# new, unique fingerprint). That tradeoff is accepted: real, present
# security is more important than avoiding a rare future manual step.
